Malcolm Ryan wrote:
> azgor wrote:
> > [9]: Give puddings a "critical mass".  When there are too many in one
> > place, they go BOOM!!, creating holes, and tears in the fabric of
> > space-time (magic portals and level teleporters).  Oh, and doing
> > massive damage to any unfortunate who happens to be in the middle of it
> > all....
>
> That sounds kind of arbitrary to me. They're just blobs of goo, after
> all.
>
> How about this for an anti-pudding farming measure? Puddings recombine.
> Two neighbouring puddings have a chance of combining back into one
> bigger pudding. You could even make it so the the combined pudding was
> stronger than before. That would make farming a much riskier business.
>
> Personally, though, I still think that pudding farming is interesting
> behaviour for the game, and shouldn't be penalised. At worst, we could
> remove death-drops for puddings, so that they only ever leave corpses.

I'd like to see death-drops eliminated altogether.  Instead, create the
item when the monster is generated and put it in its inventory.  It
seems silly for a dwarf to drop a nice offensive wand that it couldn't
use because it wasn't created until the dwarf died.

And then just don't give monsters generated by a split any starting
inventory.  I don't see any problem with farming puddings for
sacrifice.  The fact that puddings divide is just another interesting
behavior for the game, one which can kill a character of be used to an
advantage.

> > [11]: A "wait until fully healed or disturbed" command. Maybe if you do
> > n1234. to wait a long time it stops waiting when you become fully
> > healed. "You feel fully recovered. You stop waiting."
>
> This would be very useful. Incidentally n1234s is a better way to wait.

This would be nice, but it's not really a big deal to guess roughly how
many turns you need and wait that long.  I usually try to underestimate
by a bit, and usually get to within a few hp of max after 2-4 tries,
unless I'm interrupted.



